---
scope: src/service/install/provisionBrain
description: "Provisions the brain layer at install time by scaffolding the user-owned brain config from the bundled template and planning the brain MCP entry derived from it."
intent: >
  provisionBrain is the install category that stands up the brain layer
  on a host. It scaffolds the brain config file from the bundled
  template into the user-owned configuration directory, leaving every
  unwired slot as the structural unwired-slot signal so the user
  finishes wiring later. It then plans the brain MCP server entry
  from the scaffolded brain config, never from a launcher constant. The
  bundled template is written for Obsidian and is the default way to satisfy the required-section contract and repair's
  only source of current section text.
  The category also self-heals whenever the brain config is missing.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The brain config is scaffolded from the bundled template at first install, with unwired slots left as the structural unwired-slot signal so the user finishes wiring after install. The default template targets Obsidian, and a user-authored config for any other backend that satisfies the required-section contract is equally valid.
    - "Brain config idempotency is presence-only: once the file lands inside the user-owned configuration directory the user owns it forever, customizations are never proposed for overwrite, and a missing file self-heals on the next call."
    - The brain MCP entry's prescription is always derived from the scaffolded brain config — its launcher shape, its arguments, and any unwired slots flow from the config bytes through the parser and interpolator unchanged. The scaffolded config's launcher follows the launch mode chosen for the brain server, global by command name alone or local through the package runner.
    - This category plans only the brain config scaffold and the brain MCP entry. Brain entry-point artifacts and the brain root itself are seeded later by the integration's own prose, not by this category.
    - The bundled template satisfies the current required-section contract in full, so a fresh scaffold boots clean, and its section text is written for Obsidian, the backend it targets. Its kanban seeds plant a short kanban index and one guide per kanban operation, written for the per-project folder layout where a card's folder is its column, that reproduce the user's working kanban conventions exactly, so an agent reads the index plus only the operation it is performing.
    - The brain configuration command the template teaches creates a missing brain config after asking which backend and which launch mode — the bundled template for Obsidian, agent-written wiring plus user-confirmed sections for any other — and repairs an existing one by adding missing required sections and offering each section whose text is a known outdated template version, and any legacy launcher, as its own shown before/after change that is skipped unless the user says yes. Agents read the published contract, the missing-section blocks and the known outdated section versions from the latest template through the install action's read-only brain step, adapt sections with the user when the backend is not the template's, seed brain-side files for new seed sections skip-on-exists, and validate the result against the real parser before finishing.
  undesired:
    - This category writing any brain entry-point artifact, the brain root, or other user-storage content, or introspecting which brain config argument slot means what — by index, sentinel string, token-shape matcher, or declared slot kinds. Slot semantics belong to the integration's prose, never to this package.
    - Template kanban seeds that drift from the user's working kanban (card format, priorities, project linking, or the per-project folder layout), that reintroduce a board file or a plugin-specific board format, that are rewritten in backend-neutral language, or that collapse into one large guide an agent must read in full to perform a single operation.
    - "A would-overwrite step returned for any path inside the user-owned configuration directory under any rationale, including landing newer template defaults. The directory is the ownership boundary."
    - Constructing the brain MCP entry inline from a launcher constant, hardcoded command, or hardcoded argument list, or scaffolding a launcher in a mode the user did not choose or pinned to one machine's path. The scaffolded brain config is the single source of truth for launcher bytes.
    - Substituting the structural unwired-slot signal for an empty string, a literal placeholder, a fallback path, or a dropped array element anywhere along the plan path. The unwired-slot signal must reach the MCP entry prescription verbatim so the downstream write surface can refuse to land a partially-wired entry.
    - A repair that changes an existing section, the launcher wiring, or any user customization without the user's explicit yes to that one shown change, that flags a section stale on a guess when its text matches no known outdated template version, or that draws section text from anywhere but the latest bundled template. Repair never replaces a customized section as stale, never inserts Obsidian-specific text unadapted into another backend's config, and the runtime brain tool never returns seed bytes.
---
